mio waits with poll(2) on Radix and wakes a poll through a pipe, as on
the Hurd: Radix has no epoll or kqueue. Its socket addresses are Linux's,
without the Hurd's length fields.

poll(2)'s selector takes a descriptor that hung up out of the poll, as it
must, or poll would report it again and again; its deregistration then
found it gone and failed, and tokio keeps the I/O resource of a source
whose deregistration fails (tokio's runtime/io/driver.rs), so every pipe
or socket that hung up while registered was kept for as long as the
program ran: a command's output and errors in each run of admind's
worker. The selector now remembers a descriptor that hung up until it is
deregistered, which then succeeds, or until its number is registered
again.

